@startuml fig_index_vs_hotrow
!include _style.puml
' Book figure — an index changes how many rows are touched; a hot row changes who may touch one.
' Form: structure. Evidence status: conceptual illustration; asserts no rate.
title two different tools: the index narrows the scan, the hot row serialises the writers
top to bottom direction
skinparam nodesep 16
skinparam ranksep 24

rectangle "INDEX — how many rows the engine touches" as P1 #FBFBF6 {
  rectangle "query\na donor's donations" as Q
  rectangle "index on donor_id" as I <<derived>>
  rectangle "few matching rows" as R <<authoritative>>
  Q --> I : lookup
  I --> R : narrow path
}
note bottom of P1
  The index is a copy of an ordering; it taxes
  every write to the indexed column.
end note

rectangle "HOT ROW — who may touch one row at once" as P2 #FBFBF6 {
  rectangle "writer 1" as W1
  rectangle "writer 2" as W2
  rectangle "writer 3" as W3
  rectangle "one contended row\n(e.g. a counter or stock row)" as HR <<authoritative>>
  W1 --> HR
  W2 --> HR
  W3 --> HR
}
note bottom of P2
  Every writer serialises on the same row.
  Arbitration - a conditional update, a lock
  plus recheck, or pre-created units - is the
  design question, not structure.
end note

R -[hidden]down-> W1
@enduml
